{
  "id": "modality-effect",
  "term": "modality effect",
  "definition": "In numerical cognition, the modality effect refers to a change in performance, such as reaction time, depending on the form in which a number was presented, such as an Arabic digit versus a written number word. In this context, such an effect suggests that numbers may be stored or retrieved in notation-specific memory representations rather than through a single fully abstract format.",
